.page patchouli-pager 1 look at a text one screen at a time
.alias less more

# SYNOPSIS
*patchouli-pager* [*-NSRFIe*] [*-p* _PATTERN_] [*--title* _TEXT_] [*+*_LINE_ | *+/*_PATTERN_] [_FILE_...]
*less* ...
*more* ...

# DESCRIPTION
The pager shows the _FILE_s, or standard input if there are none, a screen at a time. It is what `patchouli` hands the manual pages to, what `PAGER` names in the login profile, and what `less` and `more` start. The three names are the same program; run as `more` it quits when you scroll past the end and the status line reads `--More--(NN%)`.

If standard output is not a terminal the pager behaves like `cat` (with `-N` it numbers the lines). When the text comes from a pipe, the keys are read from the terminal.

The bottom line shows the name, the lines on the screen, how far into the text you are, and `(END)` at the end. The input is read as it is needed, so `yes | patchouli-pager` starts at once. Searches are extended regular expressions (see regex(7)); a pattern that is not valid is searched for as plain text. Case is ignored unless the pattern has an upper case letter.

# OPTIONS
@ *-N*
    Number the lines.
@ *-S*
    Chop long lines instead of wrapping them; the left and right arrows scroll sideways.
@ *-R*
    Pass ANSI colour and attribute sequences through (otherwise they are shown as visible escape characters). The manual viewer uses this.
@ *-F*
    Quit at once, printing the text, if it fits on one screen.
@ *-e*
    Quit when the end of the text is reached and a further forward command is given.
@ *-I*
    Always ignore case in searches.
@ *-p* _PATTERN_, *+/*_PATTERN_
    Start at the first line that matches.
@ *+*_LINE_
    Start at that line.
@ *--title* _TEXT_
    Show _TEXT_ instead of the file name in the status line.
@ *-V*, *-h*
    Version; usage.

Options are also read from the environment variable `LESS`. Many of the other options of `less` are accepted and ignored.

# KEYS
Most commands take a count typed first: `10j`, `50g`, `5f`.

@ SPACE, f, ^F, PgDn
    Forward one screen.
@ b, ^B, PgUp
    Back one screen.
@ ENTER, j, e, ^N, Down; y, k, ^P, Up
    Forward / back one line.
@ d, ^D; u, ^U
    Forward / back half a screen.
@ g, <, Home; G, >, End
    The beginning / the end of the text. With a count, line number _N_.
@ p, %
    Go to a percentage of the text (`50p`).
@ /_PATTERN_, ?_PATTERN_
    Search forward / backward. A count finds the _N_th match.
@ n, N
    Repeat the search in the same / the opposite direction.
@ Right, Left
    Scroll sideways (with `-S`).
@ -N, -S, -I, -R
    Type a dash and the letter to switch that option on or off.
@ =, ^G
    Show the file name and the position.
@ :n, :p
    The next and the previous file.
@ r, ^L
    Repaint the screen.
@ h, H
    Show the keys.
@ q, Q, ZZ, :q
    Quit. ^C quits too.

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if a file could not be read.

# ENVIRONMENT
@ LESS
    Default options.
@ LINES, COLUMNS
    Override the screen size reported by the console.

# EXAMPLES
    patchouli-pager /Hakurei/etc/motd
    dmesg | less
    grep -rn TODO . | more
    less +/gapsh /Hakurei/etc/profile

# SEE ALSO
patchouli(1), cat(1), head(1), tail(1), regex(7), keys(7)
